#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# casoratian

#% text
# `casoratian([f1, ..., fk], m)` gives the Casoratian: the determinant of the matrix with rows `fi(m + j)`, j = 0, ..., k - 1.
# `casoratian(eq, y, m)` gives the Casoratian of a fundamental set of the linear homogeneous difference equation `eq` of order k.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the discrete analogue of the Wronskian: a Casoratian that vanishes for every m shows that the sequences are linearly dependent.

#% item
# For an equation `a_k(m) y(m + k) + ... + a_0(m) y(m) == 0` the fundamental set is the one with the initial values `y_i(j) = kronecker_delta(i, j)`, j = 0, ..., k - 1, so the value at m = 0 is 1; by Abel's lemma `C(m + 1) = (-1)^k a_0(m)/a_k(m) C(m)`, and C is the product of these ratios. Another fundamental set has a constant multiple of it.

#% item
# The product is given in closed form for constant, rational, factorial and periodic (`mod(m, p)`) coefficients. When `a_0` or `a_k` vanishes at one of the first indices the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
casoratian([2^m, 3^m], m)
#% output
#= 2^m*3^m

#% cell
casoratian([m, m^2, m^3], m)
#% output
#= 2*m*(1 + m)*(2 + m)

#% cell
casoratian([m^2, 3*m^2 + 5*m + 1, m^2 + 5*m + 1], m)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
casoratian(y(m + 2) == y(m + 1) + y(m), y, m)
#% output
#= (-1)^m

#% cell
casoratian(y(m + 2) - (m + 5)*y(m) == 0, y, m)
#% output
#= pochhammer(5, m)*(-1)^m

#% cell
casoratian(y(m + 2) - factorial(m)*y(m) == 0, y, m)
#% output
#= barnes_g(1 + m)*(-1)^m

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `wronskian`, `r_solve`, `det`
